          "text": "# SV-MATH-001 \u2014 ALLOW-Gate Admissibility in the GCAT/BCAT Engine\n\n## 1. Categorical engine model\n\n### Definition 1.1 \u2014 Geometric category\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf{GCAT}\\) be an adhesive category. Thus:\n\n1. \\(\\mathbf{GCAT}\\) has pullbacks;\n2. pushouts along monomorphisms exist;\n3. such pushouts are Van Kampen squares.\n\nThe intended examples are typed graphs, attributed graphs, cell complexes with suitable morphisms, or other adhesive geometric structures.\n\nLet\n\n\\[\n\\partial:\\mathbf{GCAT}\\longrightarrow \\mathbf{Bd}\n\\]\n\nbe a boundary-observation functor into a category \\(\\mathbf{Bd}\\). Assume that \\(\\partial\\) preserves the pushouts used by the rewriting engine.\n\nAn object \\(X\\in\\mathbf{GCAT}\\) has observed geometric boundary \\(\\partial X\\).\n\n---\n\n### Definition 1.2 \u2014 BCAT and divergence\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf{BCAT}\\) be a category equipped with an extended divergence\n\n\\[\n\\Delta:\\operatorname{Ob}(\\mathbf{BCAT})\\times\n\\operatorname{Ob}(\\mathbf{BCAT})\n\\longrightarrow [0,\\infty].\n\\]\n\nAssume:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta(B,B)=0\n\\]\n\nand, where required, the triangle inequality\n\n\\[\n\\Delta(B_0,B_2)\n\\leq\n\\Delta(B_0,B_1)+\\Delta(B_1,B_2).\n\\]\n\nLet\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:\\mathbf{GCAT}\\longrightarrow \\mathbf{BCAT}\n\\]\n\nbe the behavioral interpretation functor.\n\nThe engine assigns each rewrite rule \\(p\\) a finite divergence budget\n\n\\[\n\\kappa(p)\\in[0,\\infty).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Definition 1.3 \u2014 Ontological interpretation\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf{Ont}\\) be a category of ontology types and ontology-preserving maps, and let\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal O:\\mathbf{GCAT}\\longrightarrow\\mathbf{Ont}\n\\]\n\nbe the ontology interpretation functor.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal W\\) be a class of ontology-consistent spans in \\(\\mathbf{Ont}\\), closed under isomorphism and under the pushouts used by the engine.\n\nA span\n\n\\[\nA \\xleftarrow{u} C \\xrightarrow{v} B\n\\]\n\nis ontology-consistent precisely when it belongs to \\(\\mathcal W\\).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Categorical rewriting\n\n### Definition 2.1 \u2014 Rewrite rule\n\nA GCAT rewrite rule is a span of monomorphisms\n\n\\[\np=\n\\left(\nL \\xleftarrow{\\,l\\,} K \\xrightarrow{\\,r\\,} R\n\\right).\n\\]\n\nHere:\n\n- \\(L\\) is the left-hand side;\n- \\(R\\) is the right-hand side;\n- \\(K\\) is the preserved interface;\n- \\(L-l(K)\\) is deleted;\n- \\(R-r(K)\\) is created.\n\nA match of \\(p\\) in a host object \\(X\\) is a monomorphism\n\n\\[\nm:L\\hookrightarrow X.\n\\]\n\nA raw rewrite candidate is a pair\n\n\\[\nc=(p,m).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Definition 2.2 \u2014 DPO executability\n\nThe candidate \\(c=(p,m)\\) is **structurally executable**, written\n\n\\[\nE(c),\n\\]\n\nwhen there exist objects and morphisms making the following two squares pushouts:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\nK & \\xrightarrow{l} & L\\\\\n{\\scriptstyle k}\\downarrow && \\downarrow{\\scriptstyle m}\\\\\nD & \\xrightarrow{d} & X\n\\end{array}\n\\qquad\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\nK & \\xrightarrow{r} & R\\\\\n{\\scriptstyle k}\\downarrow && \\downarrow{\\scriptstyle n}\\\\\nD & \\xrightarrow{h} & Y.\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{DPO}\n\\]\n\nIn that case the engine has a double-pushout derivation\n\n\\[\nX\\;\\Rightarrow_{p,m}\\;Y.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathbf{GCAT"
